---
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "What Internet Speed Do You Need for Remote Work?";
const description =
  "Find out exactly what internet speed you need for remote work. Speed requirements for Zoom, Teams, cloud apps, file uploads, and tips for better connections.";
const author = "FloatJet Team";
const authorUrl = "/about";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-24");
const readingTime = 7;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "WiFi router and laptop representing internet speed for remote work";

// Table of contents headings
const headings = [
  { text: "Quick Answer", slug: "quick-answer", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Speed Requirements by Task", slug: "requirements", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Download vs Upload", slug: "download-upload", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bandwidth vs Latency", slug: "bandwidth-latency", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Multiple People in Your Household", slug: "multiple-users", depth: 2 },
  { text: "How to Test Your Speed", slug: "test-speed", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Improving Your Connection", slug: "improve", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Mobile Hotspot Backup", slug: "hotspot", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Questions", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Minimum speed for Zoom?",
    answer:
      '<p><a href="https://support.zoom.com/hc/en/article?id=zm_kb&sysparm_article=KB0060649" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Official requirements say six hundred kbps for one-on-one, one meg for groups, three point eight for HD</a> according to Zoom. Real world? Aim for ten down and five up minimum so quality doesn\'t tank when something else happens on your network.</p>',
  },
  {
    question: "Video choppy even with fast internet?",
    answer:
      "<p>Speed isn't always the problem. High latency causes delay and sync issues. Packet loss drops data. WiFi interference. Old router hardware. ISP throttling specific traffic types. Test latency and packet loss, not just speed.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need fiber?",
    answer:
      "<p>Not necessarily. Fiber is great but cable works for most remote work. DSL can work if you're getting twenty-five plus meg. Five G home internet is fast but latency varies. What matters: consistent twenty-five plus down, ten plus up, low latency. Technology matters less than actual performance.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How much does VPN slow things down?",
    answer:
      "<p>Ten to thirty percent typical. Up to fifty on bad servers. Latency adds twenty to fifty ms depending on server distance. If you need fifty meg for work and use VPN, get seventy plus base speed.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Five G hotspot good enough?",
    answer:
      "<p>Can be. Speeds run hundred to three hundred plus in good coverage. Latency is fifteen to thirty ms usually. Reliability varies by location. Data caps are the main limitation. Works for short-term or backup. For all-day work, verify coverage and consider unlimited plans.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "WiFi or ethernet?",
    answer:
      "<p>Ethernet is better when possible. More stable. Lower latency. Consistent speeds versus WiFi fluctuations. If ethernet isn't practical, optimize WiFi. Central router placement, five gigahertz band, minimize interference, mesh systems for larger spaces.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I know if ISP is throttling?",
    answer:
      "<p>Symptoms: specific apps slow while speed tests look fine. Test by using VPN and comparing speeds. VPN hides traffic type from your ISP. If speeds are significantly better with VPN, they may be throttling specific traffic. Slower during peak evening hours suggests congestion or throttling too.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What speed for screen sharing?",
    answer:
      "<p>More upload than you'd expect. Basic sharing needs one to two meg upload. High quality needs three to five. Sharing plus video simultaneously needs five to eight. If you screen share frequently, ensure at least ten meg upload.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere",
    excerpt:
      "Tips for finding fast, secure internet connections as a remote worker or digital nomad.",
    href: "/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "WiFi signal and laptop",
  },
  {
    title: "Best VPN for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Secure your connection and access content from anywhere.",
    href: "/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1614064641938-3bbee52942c7-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "VPN security illustration",
  },
  {
    title: "VPN and Public WiFi Security Guide",
    excerpt: "Stay safe when working from coffee shops, airports, and coworking spaces.",
    href: "/blog/vpn-public-wifi-guide",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1510915228340-29c85a43dcfe-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Public WiFi security",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Productivity"
    categorySlug="guides/productivity"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    Dropped from a client call mid-sentence last week. Not because my internet was slow. It was actually fine, eighty
    meg down. The problem? Three meg upload. Didn't even know that was my weak spot until the call crashed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most people obsess over download speed. Makes sense. That's what ISPs advertise. But for remote work? Upload matters
    way more than you'd think.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the short version if you're busy: fifty down, ten up handles most remote work fine. If you share your
    connection with family or roommates, double it.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Answer -->
  <h2 id="quick-answer">Quick Answer</h2>
  <p>
    For most remote workers:
  </p>
  <div class="bg-jet-stream/20 border border-jet-stream/30 rounded-lg p-4 my-6">
    <p class="text-white mb-2"><strong>Minimum that actually works:</strong> twenty-five down, five up. You'll survive.
    </p>
    <p class="text-white mb-2"><strong>Recommended:</strong> fifty down, ten up. Video calls, cloud apps, file
      transfers. No stress.</p>
    <p class="text-white"><strong>Power user territory:</strong> hundred plus down, twenty plus up. Large files.
      Multiple streams. You do a lot.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The "recommended" tier handles daily stuff without hiccups. If your partner also works from home or you're
    constantly moving big files, go higher.
  </p>

  <!-- Speed Requirements by Task -->
  <h2 id="requirements">Speed Requirements by Task</h2>
  <p>
    Different stuff needs different bandwidth. Here's what actually matters:
  </p>

  <h3>Video Calls</h3>
  <p>
    One-on-one calls need about one and a half meg both directions. Group calls with everyone's video on? Three meg each
    way. HD with screen sharing? Five meg.
  </p>
  <p>
    But here's the thing. Those are minimums. Your connection fluctuates. Random background stuff happens. Aim for two
    to three times the minimum so you're not the person with the frozen face in every meeting.
  </p>

  <h3>Cloud Apps</h3>
  <p>
    Google Docs, Notion, Asana? Five meg is plenty. Design tools like Figma? Ten to twenty for smooth performance.
    Development stuff with constant syncing? Ten to twenty-five depending how often you're pushing code.
  </p>

  <h3>File Transfers</h3>
  <p>
    This is where upload speed really bites people.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hundred meg file at five meg upload? Two and a half minutes.<br/>
    Same file at twenty meg upload? Forty seconds.<br/>
    Gig-sized file at five meg upload? Twenty-seven minutes. Hope you didn't need that anytime soon.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you send large files regularly, check your upload speed specifically. Most people never do until they're waiting
    forever for something to sync.
  </p>

  <h3>VPN Overhead</h3>
  <p>
    Using a VPN for work? Knock off ten to thirty percent from whatever speed you have.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twenty-five meg base becomes eighteen to twenty-two effective.<br/>
    Fifty meg base becomes thirty-five to forty-five.<br/>
    Hundred meg base becomes seventy to ninety.
  </p>
  <p>
    Account for this if your company requires VPN.
  </p>

  <!-- Download vs Upload -->
  <h2 id="download-upload">Download vs Upload</h2>
  <p>
    Most home plans are asymmetric. Fast download, slower upload. ISPs love advertising the big download number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Problem is, remote work hammers upload.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Download matters when:</strong> watching someone else share their screen, pulling files from cloud storage,
    loading web apps, receiving other people's video.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Upload matters when:</strong> sharing YOUR screen (upload is critical here), sending YOUR video in calls,
    uploading files to cloud storage, backing up anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many remote workers discover upload is their actual bottleneck. Cable internet might offer hundred plus down but
    only ten to twenty up. That asymmetry causes problems.
  </p>

  <!-- Bandwidth vs Latency -->
  <h2 id="bandwidth-latency">Bandwidth vs Latency</h2>
  <p>
    Speed isn't everything. Latency affects real-time stuff way more than people realize.
  </p>

  <h3>What's Latency</h3>
  <p>
    Time for data to travel from you to a server and back. Measured in milliseconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under fifty ms: Excellent. Video calls feel natural.<br/>
    Fifty to hundred: Good. Slight delays but workable.<br/>
    Hundred to two hundred: Noticeable. People talking over each other constantly.<br/>
    Over two hundred: Frustrating. Real-time communication becomes painful.
  </p>

  <h3>Why This Matters</h3>
  <p>
    You could have gigabit speed. But if latency is three hundred ms, video calls will suck. Data arrives fast once it
    starts but there's a delay before anything starts.
  </p>

  <h3>Improving Latency</h3>
  <p>
    Use ethernet instead of WiFi. Choose VPN servers close to you geographically. Pause downloads during important
    calls. Cheap routers can add latency too.
  </p>

  <!-- Multiple Users -->
  <h2 id="multiple-users">Multiple People in Your Household</h2>
  <p>
    Living with others? Multiply your needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    One remote worker: fifty down, ten up.<br/>
    Two remote workers: hundred down, twenty up.<br/>
    Family with two workers plus kids streaming: two hundred down, thirty up.<br/>
    Multiple heavy users: three hundred plus down, fifty plus up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your router's QoS settings can help prioritize work traffic. Prioritize video conferencing apps. Limit streaming
    during work hours if needed.
  </p>

  <!-- Test Speed -->
  <h2 id="test-speed">How to Test Your Speed</h2>
  <p>
    Don't trust what your ISP advertises. Test it yourself.
  </p>

  <h3>Good Options</h3>
  <p>
    Speedtest.net shows download, upload, and ping. Fast.com is Netflix's test, good for real-world performance.
    Cloudflare's test includes latency and jitter. Google "speed test" works in a pinch.
  </p>

  <h3>Testing Tips</h3>
  <p>
    Test at different times. Peak hours differ from off-peak.<br/>
    Test both WiFi and ethernet to identify issues.<br/>
    Close other stuff during tests.<br/>
    Test multiple times and average it.<br/>
    If you use VPN, test with and without.
  </p>

  <h3>What to Actually Look At</h3>
  <p>
    Consistency matters. Big variations between tests indicate problems.<br/>
    Upload speed. Often your bottleneck.<br/>
    Latency. Under fifty ms is ideal.<br/>
    Jitter. Variation in latency. Lower is better.
  </p>

  <!-- Improving Connection -->
  <h2 id="improve">Improving Your Connection</h2>
  <p>
    Before upgrading your plan:
  </p>

  <h3>Hardware Stuff</h3>
  <p>
    Ethernet beats WiFi for stability and latency.<br/>
    Old routers bottleneck fast connections. Upgrade if yours is ancient.<br/>
    Mesh systems help in larger spaces.<br/>
    WiFi six handles multiple devices better.
  </p>

  <h3>WiFi Optimization</h3>
  <p>
    Use the five gigahertz band. Faster, less interference.<br/>
    Put router centrally. Minimize distance and walls.<br/>
    Keep it away from microwaves, baby monitors, other electronics.<br/>
    Change WiFi channel if neighbors are congesting yours.
  </p>

  <h3>Network Management</h3>
  <p>
    Schedule big downloads outside work hours.<br/>
    Pause cloud backups during video calls.<br/>
    Limit other devices. Streaming and gaming eat bandwidth.<br/>
    Enable QoS to prioritize work apps.
  </p>

  <!-- Mobile Hotspot -->
  <h2 id="hotspot">Mobile Hotspot Backup</h2>
  <p>
    Internet goes out. Having backup keeps you productive.
  </p>

  <h3>Options</h3>
  <p>
    Phone tethering. Use your data plan via WiFi or USB.<br/>
    Dedicated hotspot device with its own plan.<br/>
    Five G home internet from T-Mobile, Verizon. Works as primary or backup.
  </p>

  <h3>What to Check</h3>
  <p>
    Data limits. Unlimited plans often throttle after certain usage.<br/>
    Coverage in your work area.<br/>
    Speed. LTE typically ten to fifty meg. Five G runs fifty to three hundred plus.<br/>
    Latency. Mobile usually runs higher than wired.
  </p>

  <h3>Hotspot Tips</h3>
  <p>
    Test your hotspot before you actually need it.<br/>
    Know your limits and current usage.<br/>
    Position near a window for better signal.<br/>
    USB tethering is most stable.<br/>
    Keep phone plugged in. Hotspot kills battery fast.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Wrap Up</h2>
  <p>
    Don't overpay for speed you won't use. But don't underestimate what you actually need either.
  </p>
  <p>
    Solo remote worker: fifty down, ten up handles it.<br/>
    Multiple remote workers at home: hundred plus down, twenty plus up.<br/>
    Moving large files regularly: prioritize upload speed specifically.<br/>
    Video quality problems: check latency, not just speed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test your current connection. Find your bottleneck. Upgrade only if necessary. A reliable fifty meg connection beats
    an inconsistent two hundred meg one every time.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
